data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique that modifies living organisms' genomes, based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- A Cas9 nuclease complexed with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA) is delivered into a cell, which cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable \"molecular scissors\" that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In prokaryotes, CRISPR sequences come from bacteriophage DNA and are used to detect and destroy similar phage DNA in later infections [2].\n- Reported uses include treating diseases [4] and eliminating HIV in cell culture [5], but these sources give only headlines, so the details are unverified."}

